these racks and shelves contain a lot of  books for thousands of years we humans  have been pretty good at the whole  reading thing and in most places we do  it the same way we start with our eyes  on one side of the page and we move them  across to the other a new company called  spritz kind of looking to change all of  that instead of presenting words and  sentences that our eyes scan over it  presents words sequentially one after  another why would you want to read this  way well there may be two benefits for  starters if you're presenting things  word by word you don't actually need a  very large display to read longer text  and in fact Samsung has hooked up with  spritz to include that technology on its  latest line of smartwatches but there  may be a second benefit eighty percent  of our time reading is actually spent  moving our eyes from word to word and  refocusing on each one if we don't have  to do that anymore in this case the  words come to us we can read sprit says  aster according to the company's  research people using spritz often  settle in at around 350 to 400 words per  minute that's about twice as fast as  normal and there are some outliers  reading it a thousand words per minute  research has shown that after about 650  words per minute comprehension starts to  fall off a cliff but in some cases it  might actually not be so bad spritz says  they want to account for 15% of all  reading within two years that's a pretty  lofty goal but the company thinks it can  make some inroads into the shorter form  reading that we all are doing no you're  probably not going to read war and peace  while using spritz but checking a  crowded inbox while you're waiting for a  train standing a quick text message on  your wrist increasingly there's a whole  body of reading that has less to do with  comprehending and digesting and more to  do with just kind of glancing at and  burning through  you
 

